column3:
The threat consists in the possibility of an intruder introducing malicious code into a discredited information system or IoT device, which can then be launched "manually" by users, automatically if a certain condition is met (the user enters the system on a certain date, etc.) or using authentication data set "by default", as well as in the following cases: the possibility of unauthorized introduction of some of the violator's own data for processing into a discredited information system, in fact, having made illegal use of other people's computing resources, and blocking the operation of the device when executing certain commands. 
This threat is caused
by:  software vulnerabilities; 
weaknesses in anti-virus protection and access control measures; 
the presence of an open Telnet port on the IoT device (only for IoT devices).
This threat can be implemented:
if the discredited user works with files coming from untrusted sources; 
if he has software installation privileges;
if the owner does not change the IoT device credentials (factory password and login)

The threat consists in the possibility of an intruder increasing their privileges in a discredited system (obtaining the privilege of discredited programs) by exploiting errors in programs and executing arbitrary code with their privileges. 
This threat is caused by weaknesses in the input and command validation mechanism, as well as access control measures. 
This threat can be implemented if: 
the discredited program has elevated privileges in the system; 
the discredited program receives input data from other programs or from the user; 
the violator has the ability to transfer data to the discredited program

The threat lies in the possibility of making a forced transition to using the BIOS/UEFI containing vulnerabilities. 
This threat is caused by weaknesses in the BIOS/UEFI software update monitoring technologies. 
When using the BIOS/UEFI update technology, the following situation may occur (the conditions that characterize the situation are listed in chronological order): 
a certain version of BIOS/UEFI is installed on the computer, for which no vulnerabilities are known at the time of its operation; 
due to some circumstances, the BIOS / UEFI undergoes the update procedure, while maintaining the previous version of the BIOS/ UEFI in case of a" rollback " of the system; 
data is published about the existence of vulnerabilities in the previous version of the BIOS/UEFI; 
the system crashes, as a result of which the current (new) version of the BIOS / UEFI becomes inoperable (for example, its integrity is violated); 
the user performs the standard procedure for restoring the system's health – performs the following operations: "rollback" of the system to the previous working state

The threat consists in the possibility of an intruder gaining access to hidden / protected directories or files through various actions on the file system (adding additional characters in the file path; accessing files that are not explicitly specified in the application window). 
This threat is caused by weaknesses in the mechanism for delimiting access to file system objects. 
The implementation of this threat is possible under the following conditions:
 the violator has access rights to some file system objects; 
no verification of user input data;
 the discredited program has too high access privileges to files that are not supposed to be processed using it

The threat lies in the possibility of an intruder changing the operating modes of the computer's hardware elements by unauthorized reconfiguration of the BIOS/UEFI, which allows: 
by changing the system bus frequency, data transmission mode over communication channels, etc. to affect the overall performance of the computer or cause malfunctions in its operation;
by lowering the input voltage, turning off cooling systems temporarily ensure that the computer is inoperable; 
by setting invalid parameters for the operation of devices (the threshold value for disconnecting the device in case of overheating, input voltage, etc.), lead to physical failure of individual hardware elements of the computer. 
This threat is caused by weaknesses in the technologies used to differentiate access to BIOS/UEFI management.
The implementation of this threat is possible if the intruder has privileges to change the corresponding BIOS/UEFI settings.

The threat consists in the possibility of unauthorized access to protected information by an intruder bypassing standard mechanisms using non-standard interfaces (including access via the command line bypassing the graphical interface).
This threat is caused by weaknesses in access control measures for protected information, and weaknesses in filtering input data. 
This threat can be implemented if the violator has:
the ability to enter arbitrary data in the address bar; 
information about the path to the protected resource; 
the ability to change the input data input interface

The threat consists in the possibility of performing destructive software impact on the discredited system or indirect destructive software impact through it on other systems by performing unauthorized access to virtual machine images. 
This threat is caused by weaknesses in access control measures for VM images implemented in the virtualization software. 
The implementation of this threat may lead to: 
violation of the confidentiality of protected information processed using virtual machines created on the basis of unauthorized modified images; 
violation of the integrity of programs installed on virtual machines; 
violation of the availability of virtual machine resources;
creation of a botnet by injecting malicious software into virtual machine images used in virtual machines. as templates (reference images)

The threat lies in the possibility of direct access (access from architecture levels lower than the operating system level) to data stored on the machine storage medium, or data recovery from residual information read from the machine storage medium. 
This threat is caused by weaknesses in the mechanism for deleting information from machine media – information deleted from machine media can be restored in most cases. 
This threat can be implemented under the following conditions: 
information was deleted from the machine storage medium without using methods (methods, algorithms) of guaranteed data erasure (for example, physical destruction of the machine storage medium); 
technological features of the machine storage medium do not lead to guaranteed destruction of information when receiving a command to erase data; 
information was not stored in cryptographically transformed form

The threat lies in the possibility of the violator changing the sequence of actions performed by discredited applications that use process management technologies based on the current time and state of the information system (for example, the current values of global variables, the presence of running processes, etc.), or in the possibility of modifying settings and changing the operating modes of industrial robots that lead to interference in the production process. process and theft of information stored in the robots ' memory (source code, product parameters, etc.). 
This threat is based on weaknesses in the synchronization and state management mechanism that allow the intruder to make changes to its operation at certain intervals, or the lack of authentication and authorization mechanisms. 
Implementation of this threat is possible if the violator has the ability to: 
monitor the status of the discredited application (algorithm execution stages) or industrial robots; 
track time points when the discredited application temporarily interrupts its work with global data; 
perform destructive actions at certain points in time (for example, make changes to the data file or change the contents of a memory location)

The threat consists in the possibility of the violator illegally withdrawing protected information from the system, as well as transmitting control commands by placing it non-standard (imperceptible, hidden) in open data legitimately transmitted over the network (or stored on alienated media) by masking it under service protocols, hiding other data in the stream (steganography) , using hidden pixels ("tracking pixels"), etc. 
This threat is caused by insufficient measures to protect information from leakage, as well as control data flows. 
This threat can be implemented if:
 the violator has rights in the discredited system to install specialized software that implements the functions of embedding their own information in data packets generated for transmission in the system; 
access to data transmission channels; 
when a user visits websites on the Internet and opens emails containing hidden pixels

The threat consists in the possibility of the intruder's indirect destructive software impact on the discredited system by a large volume of network traffic generated by third-party servers in response to network requests made by the intruder on behalf of the discredited system. The network traffic generated by third-party servers significantly exceeds the volume of network requests generated by the intruder.
This threat is caused by weaknesses in the firewall measures of the discredited information system, measures to control the authenticity of network requests on third-party servers, as well as weaknesses in the open system interaction model.
This threat can be implemented if the intruder has: 
information about third-party servers with insufficient network request authentication measures; 
information about the network address of the discredited system; 
special software that implements network packet generation functions

The threat consists in the possibility of refusing to allocate resources to a legitimate user for processing their requests due to the violator's exhaustion of free resources in the system, carried out by unauthorized exclusion of them from the general resource pool based on "resource leakage" or "resource allocation"techniques.
This threat is caused by weaknesses in the mechanism for controlling the distribution of computing resources between users, as well as measures for inter-network shielding of the discredited information system and monitoring the authenticity of network requests on third-party servers. 
This threat can be implemented if the intruder has: 
information about the format and parameters of destructive actions on the system that lead to the exclusion ("leakage" or "allocation") of free resources from the general resource pool of the discredited system; 
privileges sufficient for performing destructive actions ("leakage" or "allocation") in the discredited system; 
administrators do not have the ability to: "resource leaks" – system reboots when an intruder sends a large number of resource allocation requests, and for the "resource allocation" technique – forced release of resources allocated at the request of malicious processes

The threat is the possibility of loss of information stored on the formatted storage medium, often without the possibility of recovery, due to deliberate or accidental execution of the formatting procedure for the storage medium. 
This threat is caused by weak measures to restrict access to the system's media formatting function. 
The implementation of this threat is affected by such factors as: 
the time elapsed after formatting; 
the type of information carrier; 
the type of file system of the media;
the intensity of interaction with the media after formatting, etc.

The threat consists in the possibility of an intruder increasing privileges in systems that use a digital code signature as a link between a program and its privileges, by discrediting the program code signing mechanism.
This threat is caused by weaknesses in the program code signing mechanism.
This threat can be implemented under the following conditions: 
the discredited program code is written using a framework that supports signing program code; 
the discredited program code is signed by the vendor (software provider); 
the violator has the ability to inject program code into the discredited computer

The threat consists in the ability of an intruder to gain access rights to protected information by intercepting exceptions/signals generated by a section of program code executed with elevated privileges (a privileged function block) and containing commands for managing protected information. 
This threat is caused by the fact that calling program functions in privileged mode implies disabling access control mechanisms for them. 
This threat can be implemented under the following conditions: 
a discredited program written in a programming language that supports the privileged block mechanism (for example, Java); 
in a discredited program, privileged blocks are called in an unsafe way (public declaration of internal functions is used, exceptions are generated from the privileged block); 
the violator has sufficient rights to intercept software exceptions in the system

The threat consists in the possibility for the violator to gain unauthorized access to the information infrastructure by obtaining the violator the right to control the automated process control system that is part of it by exploiting vulnerabilities in its software or weaknesses in technological data transmission protocols. 
This threat is caused by the presence of software-based network interaction interfaces in the automated process control system and, as a result, the possibility of unauthorized access to this system, as well as insufficient network traffic filtering and anti-virus protection measures. 
The implementation of this threat is possible if the violator has the rights to interact with the automated process control system. The implementation of this threat may result in: 
blocking or distortion (incorrect execution) of algorithms for working out tasks for managing technological processes, direct control of enterprise equipment; 
violation of the normal course of technological processes; 
partial or complete shutdown of technological processes without (or with) equipment failure; 
emergency situation in a critical information infrastructure system

The threat consists in the possibility of the violator collecting and analyzing information processed using a mobile device, through the use of special software embedded by the user in the system software of the mobile device, as well as embedded in mobile programs under the guise of a software platform for their development by other companies. 
This threat is caused by the presence of multiple data transmission channels in a mobile device, as well as the complexity of controlling the flow of information in such a device. 
This threat can be implemented if users use mobile devices. The information collected may include: 
personal data of the user and persons in contact with them (gender, age, religious and political views, etc.); 
restricted access information (browser history, user's contact list, call history, etc.); 
environmental data (current location of the mobile device, driving directions, availability of wireless networks within the access radius); 
video data captured by the mobile device's video cameras; 
audio data captured by the device's microphone

The threat consists in the possibility of an intruder injecting malicious code into the user's computer when visiting infected sites. The intruder identifies the sites most visited by the user, then hacks them and inserts malicious code into them. This threat is caused by weaknesses in anti-virus protection measures, as well as the lack of firewall rules. This threat can be implemented if: 
the user has unlimited access to the Internet;
 the intruder has information about the sites visited by the user

The threat is the ability to run malicious code remotely by creating applications that bypass the security mechanisms built into the operating system. 
This threat is caused by errors in processors (for example, errors in the Intel processor of the Haswell generation), which allow the creation of special applications to bypass the protection mechanisms built into the operating system (for example, the ASLR mechanism). 
This threat can be implemented if: a collision is initiated in the target buffer table - it can be used to find out the memory areas where specific code fragments are located; 
creating an application that uses these code fragments to bypass the protection mechanism; 
run this application in conjunction with an exploit of any vulnerability of the operating system itself to create the ability to run malicious code remotely

The threat consists in the possibility of accessing protected memory from a program that does not have the appropriate rights, as a result of exploiting vulnerabilities that allow you to overcome the access control mechanism implemented by the central processor. 
The implementation of this threat is caused by the presence of vulnerabilities related to an error in memory access control based on speculative execution of processor instructions. An access control error is caused by the following factors: 
1) no verification of the process's access rights to readable areas during speculative operations, including reading from RAM; 
2) lack of clearing the cache from the results of erroneous speculative execution; 
3) storing operating system kernel data in the process address space. 
The implementation of this threat is possible due to the presence of processors with hardware vulnerabilities and the lack of relevant updates.

The threat consists in the possibility of disruption of the functioning ("circumvention") by the violator of the means that implement artificial intelligence technologies.
This threat is caused by the following reasons: 
- no necessary data in the training set;
- the presence of shortcomings in the machine learning model;
